  "title": "All and Both: omni-, pan- and ambi-",
  "short": "omni- (Latin) and pan- (Greek) mean all; ambi- means both.",
  "trouble": "pan- means all only in Greek-based words (panorama, panacea); it has nothing to do with a cooking pan.",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.numbers"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "An <i>omnivore</i> eats everything, plants and animals alike. A <i>panorama</i> is a view of everything around you. <b>omni-</b> (Latin) and <b>pan-</b> (Greek) both mean “all.” A third prefix, <b>ambi-</b>, means “both”: an <i>ambidextrous</i> person can use both hands well."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
          "scene": {
            "bg": "forest",
            "animals": [
              {
                "kind": "bear",
                "x": 46,
                "s": 1.6,
                "mood": "joy"
              }
            ],
            "props": [
              {
                "kind": "fish",
                "x": 20,
                "s": 1.7
              },
              {
                "kind": "apple",
                "x": 74,
                "s": 1.6,
                "front": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "grapes",
                "x": 86,
                "s": 1.6
              },
              {
                "kind": "carrot",
                "x": 32,
                "s": 1.5,
                "rot": 20,
                "front": true
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "text": "OMNI-VORE: eats it all",
                "x": 50,
                "y": 92
              }
            ],
            "alt": "A happy bear sits among fish, apples, grapes and carrots, ready to eat them all."
          }
        },
        "cap": "A happy bear sits among fish, apples, grapes and carrots, ready to eat them all."
      },
      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "<b>omni-</b> (Latin) and <b>pan-</b> (Greek) = <b>all</b>. <b>ambi-</b> = <b>both</b>, and sometimes <b>around</b>. The root <b>pot</b> = <b>able, powerful</b>."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "The <i>omni-</i> words describe someone or something with no limits: <b>omnipotent</b> (all-powerful: <i>omni-</i> + <i>pot</i>, able, as in <i>potent</i> and <i>potential</i>) and <b>omniscient</b> (all-knowing: <i>omni-</i> + <i>sci</i>, know). The <i>ambi-</i> words describe being pulled two ways: <i>ambivalent</i> feelings and <i>ambiguous</i> words."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The parts and their words"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "omni",
            "pan"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "omni- and pan- (all)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "ambi",
            "pot"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "ambi- (both) and pot (able, powerful)."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Words taken apart"
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Word and meaning",
          "Parts, and what they say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "<b>omnipotent</b><br>all-powerful",
            "<b>omni-</b> <i>all</i> + <b>pot</b> <i>able, powerful</i> + -ent<br>→ “all-powerful”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>omniscient</b><br>knowing everything",
            "<b>omni-</b> <i>all</i> + sci <i>know</i> + -ent<br>→ “all-knowing”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>omnivore</i></b><br>an animal that eats both plants and animals",
            "<b>omni-</b> <i>all</i> + vor <i>eat</i> + -e<br>→ “one who eats everything”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>panacea</b><br>a cure for all problems",
            "<b>pan-</b> <i>all</i> + ace <i>cure</i> + -a<br>→ “a cure for all”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>panorama</i></b><br>a wide view in every direction",
            "<b>pan-</b> <i>all</i> + orama <i>view</i><br>→ “a view of all”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>ambivalent</b><br>having mixed feelings",
            "<b>ambi-</b> <i>both</i> + val <i>be strong</i> + -ent<br>→ “strong both ways”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>ambiguous</b><br>open to more than one meaning",
            "<b>ambi-</b> <i>both ways</i> + gu <i>drive</i> + -ous<br>→ “driving both ways (two meanings)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>pandemonium</b><br>wild uproar and confusion",
            "<b>pan-</b> <i>all</i> + demon <i>spirit</i> + -ium<br>→ “all the spirits (a wild uproar)”"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "Each word, what it means now, and its parts. Words in italics are familiar words that are not on the list."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Use the parts",
        "q": "Lin was <b>ambivalent</b> about moving: excited about the new city, sad to leave her friends. What does <i>ambivalent</i> mean?",
        "steps": [
          "Take it apart: <b>ambi-</b> (both) + <b>val</b> (be strong, as in <i>valiant</i> and <i>value</i>) + <b>-ent</b>.",
          "Literally: “strong both ways,” two strong pulls at once.",
          "The sentence fits: she is pulled toward excitement and sadness."
        ],
        "a": "Having mixed feelings."
      },
